    ],
    "active_bundle": {
        "post_id": 1369,
        "timestamp": "20260705_055909",
        "image": "images/post_20260705_055909.jpg",
        "reel": "reels/reel_20260705_055909.mp4",
        "story": "images/story_20260705_055909.jpg",
        "carousel": [
            "images/carousel_20260705_055909_slide_1.jpg",
            "images/carousel_20260705_055909_slide_2.jpg",
            "images/carousel_20260705_055909_slide_3.jpg",
            "images/carousel_20260705_055909_slide_4.jpg",
            "images/carousel_20260705_055909_slide_5.jpg"
        ],
        "captions": {
            "instagram": "Stop calling it a 'cascade of intent' when you actually mean 'vague hope with extra steps.'\n\nWe love the poetry of intention. 'I manifest.' 'I set goals.' 'I visualize success.' But here's the uncomfortable truth: every cascade starts with one actual choice—not a wish, not a daydream, but a specific action taken when no one was watching.\n\nI used to think I was deeply intentional. Turns out I was just good at writing elaborate plans that never left the notebook. The real cascade? It started the day I stopped treating intention as a personality trait and started treating it like a measurable output.\n\nResearch in systems psychology shows that small, consistent inputs create far more reliable outcomes than massive, dramatic shifts. Your one decision to show up—without announcement, without content, without performance—creates more downstream change than a hundred Instagram-perfect goal-setting posts.\n\nAnd the hardest part? No one claps for the first domino.\n\nSo instead of asking yourself what you intend to create, ask what you actually did today that moved one small piece. That's where the cascade begins.\n\nShare this with someone who keeps waiting for the 'right moment.'\n\n#TheNineStitches #CognitiveBias #SelfUnderstanding #InnerWorkJourney",
            "linkedin": "We call it 'cascade of intent' to make our inaction feel sophisticated.\n\nBut here's what systems psychology actually tells us: every large outcome starts with one specific, unglamorous action—not a visualization, not a mission statement, but a real choice made when no one was watching.\n\nI know this because I spent years mistaking intention for action. I had notebooks full of strategies and zero execution. The cascade I actually wanted wasn't happening because I was waiting for motivation instead of building systems.\n\nThe shift was small: I stopped measuring success by how I felt and started measuring it by what I completed. That one change created more downstream momentum than any 'mindset work' I had ever consumed.\n\nReal systems thinking strips away the magic. It doesn't care about your dreams. It cares about your inputs—and whether they're consistent enough to trigger something meaningful downstream.\n\nTell me how this lands for you.\n\n#TheNineStitches #InnerWorkJourney #SelfUnderstanding #SelfAwarenessPractice #MindsetScience\n\nWhat's one rule you wish you'd known earlier about this?",
            "pinterest": "The most overrated currency we have is intention.\n\nWe treat intentions like achievements. 'I intended to...' as if the thought itself counts. But systems psychology shows that cascades don't start from thoughts—they start from tiny, repeated actions that compound in obscurity.\n\nStop glorifying the plan. Start tracking the first domino.\n\n#TheNineStitches #ThoughtPatterns #BehaviorPatterns #MindsetScience",
            "youtube": "We confuse intention with action as if the thought itself is the win.\n\nSystems psychology says otherwise. Cascades start with one small, specific input—not a wish, not a plan, but an actual decision made in private. The people who create real downstream change aren't the ones with the most beautiful visions. They're the ones who kept showing up when the vision was gone.\n\nI am curious what this brings up for you.\n\n#TheNineStitches #InnerWorkJourney #SelfUnderstanding #PsychologyDaily",
            "threads": "The most overrated currency? Intention.\n\nWe treat 'I intended to' as if it's an achievement. But cascades don't start from thoughts. They start from one small action taken without an audience.\n\nWhat's the last 'cascade' you actually started—not planned, but started?\n\nKeep this close for the days you need it.\n\nWant to read more?... check out my LinkedIn",
            "bluesky": "Intentions are overrated. Cascades are built from small, unglamorous actions.\n\nWhat's one small input you made today that no one saw?\n\nWant to read more?... check out my LinkedIn"
        },
